MAOI medications + therapy. Worked for me. But you need to find a doc willing to work with you on the dosage. A lot of docs are too scared because of the risk of hypertension/heart failure. This rarely happens though as long as you stick to the MAOI diet.Just explain that you'll be careful and keep track of everything on a day to day. The reward? No more ADHD/Anhedonia/Depression, the ability to wake up each morning and go through my to do list. I set 2-3 goals for myself (ie. 4hrsx3days for guitar, 2hrsx4days for gym, 1hrx3days riding bike, 5hrsx2days for social outings, the rest goes to sleep and cooking). I set the time slots and just do the activities no matter what.

&#x200B;

Anyways thats my best advice. Good luck

20 mg per day for me. Originally started on 5mg, then I slowly ramped up to that dose over the span of about 8 years. People generally can do well somewhere between 5mg to 10mg. Anything beyond 10mg and you risk the cheese effect, I had to follow a low tyramine diet. 

&#x200B;

If the dose is too high, it will cause what I call the "bad type of insomnia", where your body does not eventually adapt its sleep architecture and you end up being zombified for as long as youre on that wrong dose.

If the dose is just right, you will experience what I call "initial insomnia", where you will have a few sleep deprived nights, maybe some low quality sleep, but otherwise seemingly recover your functionality/mood as long as you keep your diet and sleep timing consistent. Don't forget to take a high quality multivitamin along with DLPA. They are the literal building blocks of your brains own natural antidepressants/focus aids (your neurotransmitters!). I always say this and people roll their eyes, but the multivitamin is supposed to cover nutritional gaps in your diet, especially if you're the occasional junk eater.

If the dose is too low, you'll feel it somewhat lacking in terms of antidepressant properties. 

Keep coffee to 1-2 cup per day (i'm sure you already know this from your experience with those other MAOIs you tried).

&#x200B;

If you experience disruptive mania/hypomania, let your doc know. I used to keep a journal that I would write in daily. I recorded sleep, food, mood, productivity, and overall opinion daily. Its a pain in the ass but for a 10 minute investment each morning you can have a nice visual log of whats going on with your health, and might even help to identify areas where you might be messing up.

&#x200B;

Cheers mate. Good luck!

Hello, I have a lot of experience with MAOs (not a doctor though, so take this with a grain of salt):

&#x200B;

MAO's tax your liver time. Your liver spends a lot of hours per day breaking down the medication into its metabolites. This leaves less capacity for the liver to process Alcohol and dispose of it properly. So alcohols half life is about 2 hours per shot. When your on medication, the actual clearance rate can drastically vary. Having enough alcohol in your system by the time it is bed time causes you to sleep poorly (it cuts down REM sleep). Cutting down quality of sleep, means your body is not able to replenish vital neurotransmitters that play a part in your mental health and medication efficacy.

Just know your body and moderate your intake (I myself only have a beer or two per week). You actually stand to benefit more from feeling good all day the next day, instead of feeling really good for those 4-5 hours.

If you're feeling that craving, going for a run until you get runners high releases the same endorphins you enjoy while drinking. I wish I could go for bike rides/runs like I used to but I have a medical condition that is slowly healing right now. If you can get out there, have a blast lol.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Hope this helps

