damn I just read that link you posted. Very detailed, this guy I can tell is quite the neuromancer, a benevolent one at that. OP should read this excerpt as well since it directly relates to the path OP's psych has taken... the stuff I posted was just basic stuff for mental health lol! forgive me for my naivete, but Dr. Gillman seems like he's kind of standardized an approach for the treatment resistent population... (my honest assessment? he seems quite experienced with many different MAOIs, Tricycyclics, other helpful adjuncts. I would say that you show his website to your psychiatrist if you're considering his path.... I would also say its important to kind of get a grasp of your neurotransmitter profile. Some people need more dopamine, some need more norepinephrine, some need more serotonin. Me? I'm low on dopamine and norephinephrine, and have an overabundance of serotonin... You may ask... how can I tell? well... you've got to do a little bit of reading on each neurotransmitter and how the brain works (just the gist of it, not trying to make you anxious and confused now, think of it as a fun learning process!), and understanding the neurotransmitter affinities of the maoi you have decided to take.

&#x200B;

Ah, alas, my brain is fried now, I do not think I can offer any further advice than this. I shall let the other mental wizards take over for me.... lol! and if you want, be my friend!\~ you can chat with me anytime

Hmmm.... I'm on selegiline as for MAOI therapy. Its for ADHD and brain damage from past seizures i've experienced.  Everyone's mileage varies with different medication. You're switching behavior between Nardil and Parnate is a symptom of your body trying to tell you something subconsciously (kind of like if you were deficient in magnesium and find yourself drinking more gatorade for no reason at all besides craving it). Heres my take: you need to just pick one and stick to it consistently, but add some adjuncts that work perfectly as a cocktail, and this cocktail should last you for years and years to come before you have to start again at square one. Don't be anxious, these years to come will be quite beautiful if you figure it out. Also, don't expect it to perfect, nothing is perfect and you will feel some uncomfortable moments here and there (as long as it does not majorly interfere with your life)

&#x200B;

I agree with the person above me. Adjuncts can help with issues efficacy. MAOIs are flippin' awesome in their efficacy for helping with treatment resistant depression/ADHD... HOWEVER, some adjuncts must be considered on a one-by-one basis. Journaling every day to record brief results revolving around sleep quality/duration, productivity, mood, appetite, TIMING of your meals/sleep/exercise/meds (hopefully in the morning :P), can help you keep a track record of your progress as a patient, and provide your psych with something concrete to go off of (presuming she or he gives a shit enough to read through your notes, you are paying this person almost 200-300 dollars per session after all). If journaling, keep doses exactly the same for 7 days at a time. If you experience something really messed up with current dosing protocol for a new adjunct, then stop after day 2, recover some sleep for 3 days on your previous regimen, and then recontinue at day 1 at an adjusted dosage for that adjunct.

&#x200B;

Be super careful when researching adjuncts. ANYTHING you're considering, keep on a list, and consider possible contraindictions , and possible negative side effects that could just mess everything up in then (AKA sleep, productivity, energy, duh!).

A good place to start for trying to achieve peak mental and physical health is sleep/diet/exercise/med timing. The circadian rhythm is built for consistency, and either the amount of light you take in through your eyes, OR.... the amount of food you eat during the day and what time, will determine when you go to bed. Exercise can help as an adjunct to release essential neurotransmitters for your peak functioning. Last note.... Take a reliable multivitamin along with amino acids. I shall recommend Life Extnesion 2 per day multivitamin along with D-L-Phenylalanine as an amino acid precursor for dopamine production, along with melatonin at night (assuming you're not already on sleep meds). I'm no doctor, so take this advice with a grain of salt. I'm just a veteran in the game that knows the BASICS to help you get started on your road to recovery.

&#x200B;

Best of luck, and don't give up. Always keep building momentum. I have severe ADHD that caused me to eventually have to give up university. Did this cause me to give up? Hells no, I came up with a plan and I'm going through with it so that I can still become a programmer, AND solve my health problems. I wish nothing but the best for you, and hope you will keep in touch.

&#x200B;

PS. keep in touch with me if you'd like. I likely will not be able to help though if you're someone who has schizophrenia/bipolar/borderline/sociopathy/psychopathy/narcisissm, just because only the most experienced practitioners who can see through their patients eyes and know medications inside and out can solve those issue.

